WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.679 --> 00:00:08.980
&gt;&gt;&gt; Florida governor and

00:00:09.114 --> 00:00:10.348
potential 2024 presidential

00:00:10.482 --> 00:00:12.550
nominee Ron Desantis is facing

00:00:12.684 --> 00:00:14.219
backlash but not just from

00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:14.819
Democrats.

00:00:14.953 --> 00:00:16.187
Instead, from within his own

00:00:16.221 --> 00:00:16.388
party.

00:00:16.521 --> 00:00:18.356
This comes after the Republican

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:19.758
characterized Russia's war in

00:00:20.191 --> 00:00:21.826
Ukraine as a territorial dispute

00:00:22.193 --> 00:00:22.794
between two countries.

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:26.264
&gt;&gt; Here to discuss, Alyssa fair

00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:27.832
griffin, CNN political

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:31.403
commentator and former trump

00:00:31.536 --> 00:00:33.338
white house director, and Ron

00:00:33.471 --> 00:00:34.773
Brownstein, senior editor at the

00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:35.206
atlantic.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.442
We're hearing from more

00:00:37.575 --> 00:00:39.544
Republicans who disagree with I

00:00:39.678 --> 00:00:42.247
would say 2023 Ron Desantis

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.516
because 2015 Ron Desantis said

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:45.250
something very different.

00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:47.786
Let's listen.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:50.855
Courtesy of the k-file, a radio

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:51.222
interview.

00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:52.691
&gt;&gt; We in the congress have been

00:00:52.824 --> 00:00:54.259
urging the president, I have

00:00:54.392 --> 00:00:56.227
been, to provide arms to

00:00:56.227 --> 00:00:56.261
Ukraine.

00:00:56.394 --> 00:00:57.696
They want to fight their good

00:00:57.829 --> 00:00:58.229
fight.

00:00:58.229 --> 00:00:59.597
They're not asking us to fight

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:00.298
it for them.

00:01:00.432 --> 00:01:01.900
And the president has

00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:03.735
steadfastly refused.

00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:05.870
And I think that's a mistake.

00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:07.505
&gt;&gt; So that's eight years ago.

00:01:07.639 --> 00:01:08.373
Why the flip?

00:01:08.506 --> 00:01:10.442
&gt;&gt; I think this was a terrible

00:01:10.575 --> 00:01:11.810
political miscalculation by

00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:14.713
governor Desantis because it was

00:01:14.846 --> 00:01:15.213
precisely that.

00:01:15.347 --> 00:01:17.215
As recently as 2015 after the

00:01:17.349 --> 00:01:18.817
invasion of crimea, he was in

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.251
favor of armor Ukraine to the

00:01:20.352 --> 00:01:22.253
teeth and criticizing the Obama

00:01:22.253 --> 00:01:22.854
administration for not doing

00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:23.154
more.

00:01:23.288 --> 00:01:24.489
This was clearly an effort to

00:01:24.622 --> 00:01:27.525
cater to the Maga

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:28.727
anti-interventionist wing of the

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:28.993
GOP.

00:01:29.127 --> 00:01:30.428
What I think he's going to be

00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:31.763
surprised to learn is that's a

00:01:31.896 --> 00:01:33.231
vast minority in the Republican

00:01:33.365 --> 00:01:33.531
party.

00:01:33.665 --> 00:01:34.833
The language I think really

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:36.534
resonates in a negative way with

00:01:36.668 --> 00:01:37.535
Republicans like myself is to

00:01:37.669 --> 00:01:39.804
say this is a territorial

00:01:39.938 --> 00:01:40.271
dispute.

00:01:40.271 --> 00:01:41.673
That language undermines and

00:01:41.806 --> 00:01:43.141
calls into question the

00:01:43.274 --> 00:01:44.909
sovereignty of Ukraine, a

00:01:45.043 --> 00:01:46.644
sovereign nation invaded by

00:01:46.778 --> 00:01:47.011
Russia.

00:01:47.145 --> 00:01:48.313
I think this is one of the first

00:01:48.446 --> 00:01:50.281
times he's actually gone a bit

00:01:50.281 --> 00:01:52.484
too far even with regard to the

00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:53.585
mainstream of the Republican

00:01:53.718 --> 00:01:55.186
party and they're realizing that

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:55.453
now.

00:01:55.587 --> 00:01:57.155
&gt;&gt; Yet, the two front-runners

00:01:57.288 --> 00:01:58.523
for Republican nominee, Donald

00:01:58.656 --> 00:02:00.291
Trump, his views and comments on

00:02:00.425 --> 00:02:02.293
Vladimir Putin and the war

00:02:02.293 --> 00:02:03.395
aren't that different from what

00:02:03.528 --> 00:02:05.830
we're hearing from Ron Desantis.

00:02:05.964 --> 00:02:07.766
It's all reassuring to hear from

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:09.000
Republicans now who are

00:02:09.134 --> 00:02:11.202
disagreeing with that and

00:02:11.336 --> 00:02:13.338
supporting Ukraine's defense and

00:02:13.471 --> 00:02:14.305
the United States in support of

00:02:14.305 --> 00:02:16.307
Ukraine for that defense itself.

00:02:16.307 --> 00:02:18.576
But if they become the nominee,

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.311
wouldn't you imagine all of that

00:02:20.311 --> 00:02:20.412
changes?

00:02:20.545 --> 00:02:22.447
How would they rally around a

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.315
candidate whose views they

00:02:24.315 --> 00:02:27.719
differ on foreign policy like

00:02:27.852 --> 00:02:29.287
this?

00:02:29.421 --> 00:02:31.122
&gt;&gt; These are the two candidates

00:02:31.256 --> 00:02:32.490
who in polls will have something

00:02:32.624 --> 00:02:34.359
like three-quarters of the total

00:02:34.492 --> 00:02:36.661
vote in a Republican primary.

00:02:36.795 --> 00:02:37.862
Both taking this position that

00:02:37.996 --> 00:02:41.833
is at odds with the really

00:02:41.966 --> 00:02:44.602
post-1952 Dwight Eisenhower

00:02:44.736 --> 00:02:45.937
beating Robert traft Republican

00:02:46.337 --> 00:02:46.371
party.

00:02:46.371 --> 00:02:49.574
I agree with most of what Alyssa

00:02:49.707 --> 00:02:49.874
said.

00:02:50.008 --> 00:02:51.242
I differ in once extent.

00:02:51.376 --> 00:02:53.378
I think this is clearly an

00:02:53.511 --> 00:02:54.646
example of Donald Trump being in

00:02:54.779 --> 00:02:55.947
Ron Desantis' head.

00:02:56.347 --> 00:02:57.348
As I said to you before, I think

00:02:57.482 --> 00:02:59.451
the core of the Desantis

00:02:59.584 --> 00:03:01.820
strategy is to offer Republican

00:03:01.953 --> 00:03:03.054
voters trumpism without trump.

00:03:03.188 --> 00:03:04.756
To basically say I will be as

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:06.391
fierce a cultural warrior as

00:03:06.524 --> 00:03:07.892
trump is against all the targets

00:03:08.026 --> 00:03:09.861
you want to smite, but I don't

00:03:09.994 --> 00:03:11.329
carry all the personal baggage

00:03:11.463 --> 00:03:12.363
of trump.

00:03:12.464 --> 00:03:14.365
And I think as part of that he

00:03:14.365 --> 00:03:16.367
is very determined not to let

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.002
trump establish a lot of

00:03:18.369 --> 00:03:20.371
daylight on many of these issues

00:03:20.371 --> 00:03:21.873
that have enormous appeal to the

00:03:22.006 --> 00:03:23.942
Maga base of the party.

00:03:24.375 --> 00:03:25.743
The risk, of course, is by doing

00:03:25.877 --> 00:03:28.613
that, you're creating dissidence

00:03:28.746 --> 00:03:29.781
both with another piece of the

00:03:29.914 --> 00:03:33.451
Republican party, and even more

00:03:33.585 --> 00:03:35.086
fundamentally, if you win the

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.688
nomination, I can't imagine that

00:03:36.821 --> 00:03:38.389
Joe Biden would be worried about

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:39.924
going into a general election

00:03:40.058 --> 00:03:41.926
basically standing up as the

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.028
defending of the western

00:03:44.395 --> 00:03:46.598
alliance to rebuff Vladimir

00:03:46.731 --> 00:03:48.533
Putin against an opponent who is

00:03:48.666 --> 00:03:50.401
calling this a territorial

00:03:50.401 --> 00:03:50.535
dispute.

00:03:50.668 --> 00:03:53.137
&gt;&gt; Alyssa, before we get to the

00:03:53.271 --> 00:03:54.572
nomination, do you think this

00:03:54.706 --> 00:03:55.773
language, this position from

00:03:55.907 --> 00:03:58.409
Desantis is enough to alienate

00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:01.179
some of those never trump or

00:04:01.312 --> 00:04:02.747
trump reluctant Republicans who

00:04:02.881 --> 00:04:04.516
were looking for a strong

00:04:04.649 --> 00:04:04.916
alternative?

00:04:05.049 --> 00:04:06.985
&gt;&gt; I think it's significant

00:04:07.118 --> 00:04:08.419
enough in the context of history

00:04:08.419 --> 00:04:09.954
of the moment that he's going to

00:04:10.088 --> 00:04:11.322
have to clean it up.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:13.057
A voter like myself, I'm not

00:04:13.191 --> 00:04:14.425
going to support someone who

00:04:14.526 --> 00:04:15.994
won't support continuing aid to

00:04:16.427 --> 00:04:16.461
Ukraine.

00:04:16.461 --> 00:04:17.662
The point Desantis makes is

00:04:17.795 --> 00:04:19.497
China is the biggest U.S.

00:04:19.631 --> 00:04:20.431
Adversary right now.

00:04:20.431 --> 00:04:21.866
I don't dispute that, but he

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.435
argues we can't walk and chew

00:04:24.435 --> 00:04:25.670
gum, support our Ukrainian

00:04:25.803 --> 00:04:27.772
allies while countering China.

00:04:27.906 --> 00:04:31.442
I disagree with that outright.

00:04:31.576 --> 00:04:33.044
I think we can do both of those

00:04:33.177 --> 00:04:36.514
and frankly a victorious Putin

00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:38.449
is a boon to China.

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:40.451
I imagine he has to clarify this

00:04:40.451 --> 00:04:41.986
at some point, before it gets

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.755
closer and closer to a

00:04:43.888 --> 00:04:44.455
nomination.

00:04:44.455 --> 00:04:45.657
&gt;&gt; Ron, a two-pronged question.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.859
How many Republicans agree with

00:04:47.992 --> 00:04:49.561
Alyssa's view here, and in

00:04:49.694 --> 00:04:50.695
general, historically, when

00:04:50.828 --> 00:04:53.698
you're talking about a general

00:04:53.831 --> 00:04:55.633
election, how much of a priority

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:56.734
is foreign policy, especially

00:04:56.868 --> 00:05:00.538
when it's not the United States

00:05:00.672 --> 00:05:02.073
directly at war?

00:05:02.473 --> 00:05:04.609
&gt;&gt; So first question, generally

00:05:04.742 --> 00:05:07.745
still a majority of Republicans

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:09.113
say they support aid to Ukraine,

00:05:09.247 --> 00:05:11.149
but that number is steadily

00:05:11.282 --> 00:05:11.516
declining.

00:05:11.649 --> 00:05:13.585
I'm guessing if there are polls,

00:05:13.718 --> 00:05:15.320
more recent polls than what we

00:05:15.453 --> 00:05:16.788
have seen earlier in January or

00:05:16.921 --> 00:05:19.591
so, we might be at majority

00:05:19.724 --> 00:05:20.725
opposition because they're

00:05:20.858 --> 00:05:21.960
getting such a clear cue from

00:05:22.093 --> 00:05:24.495
these party leader figures.

00:05:24.562 --> 00:05:25.763
It's a big divide in the

00:05:25.897 --> 00:05:26.497
Republican party.

00:05:26.598 --> 00:05:27.832
You have seen that in the way in

00:05:27.966 --> 00:05:29.067
which people like Chris Christie

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.569
have explicitly compared what

00:05:31.703 --> 00:05:36.507
Desantis said to appeasement, by

00:05:36.507 --> 00:05:38.543
neveral Chamberland of Adolph

00:05:38.676 --> 00:05:40.044
hitber before World War II.

00:05:40.178 --> 00:05:44.048
That's explosive to put into an

00:05:44.182 --> 00:05:44.816
intraparty debate.

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:48.720
This echoes seven decades ago on

00:05:48.853 --> 00:05:49.988
whether the focus should be

00:05:50.121 --> 00:05:51.255
defending Europe against Russia

00:05:51.389 --> 00:05:53.458
or an Asia first foreign policy,

00:05:53.591 --> 00:05:55.293
which Desantis is kind of going

00:05:55.426 --> 00:05:56.527
toward as well.

00:05:56.561 --> 00:05:57.862
I don't think foreign policy

00:05:57.996 --> 00:05:59.631
historically has been the

00:05:59.764 --> 00:06:01.666
central fulcrum of presidential

00:06:01.799 --> 00:06:02.967
elections except when we're

00:06:03.101 --> 00:06:04.969
actively at war, but it goes

00:06:05.103 --> 00:06:06.804
into the overall equation of

00:06:06.938 --> 00:06:08.106
whether you believe, a, someone

00:06:08.539 --> 00:06:10.608
is up to the job, and B, what is

00:06:10.742 --> 00:06:12.543
the real fault line now, whether

00:06:12.543 --> 00:06:14.045
someone shares your values.

00:06:14.178 --> 00:06:15.480
And I look at Desantis doing

00:06:15.613 --> 00:06:17.915
this in the context of him also

00:06:18.049 --> 00:06:20.084
pushing likely for a six-week

00:06:20.218 --> 00:06:21.953
abortion ban, for permless gun

00:06:22.086 --> 00:06:23.221
carry in Florida, and he's

00:06:23.354 --> 00:06:24.689
taking a lot of steps -- he

00:06:24.822 --> 00:06:26.557
could be a formidable candidate

00:06:26.691 --> 00:06:28.092
to help Republicans regain some

